{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.CorrectedTrapezoid","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.CorrectedTrapezoid","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.TrapezoidRule"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.abs_corrected_trapezoid_sub_integral_le_osc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.abs_corrected_trapezoid_sub_integral_le_osc","kind":"theorem","line":475,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/CorrectedTrapezoid.lean#L475-L501","doc":"**The order-2 error bound driven by the oscillation of `g″`.**\n\n    |trapezoid − B₂-correction − ∫| ≤ (b−a)³·D/12   when |g″ − g″(a)| ≤ D.\n\nCompare `abs_corrected_trapezoid_sub_integral_le`, which needs `sup|g⁗|`.  This\nversion needs only how much `g″` MOVES across the cell, which is what telescopes\nwhen `g″` is monotone. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.abs_integral_weight_sq_deriv2_mul_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.abs_integral_weight_sq_deriv2_mul_le","kind":"theorem","line":416,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/CorrectedTrapezoid.lean#L416-L473","doc":"**`|∫_a^b w″·g″| ≤ 2(b−a)³·D`** when `|g″ − g″(a)| ≤ D` on the cell.\n\nThe point is that `D` is an OSCILLATION, not a supremum: `∫ w″ = 0` lets `g″` be\nreplaced by `g″ − g″(a)` for free. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.integral_eq_corrected_trapezoid_sub_deriv2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.integral_eq_corrected_trapezoid_sub_deriv2","kind":"theorem","line":366,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/CorrectedTrapezoid.lean#L366-L414","doc":"**The order-2 identity from the order-1 one — only `C²` needed.**\n\n    ∫_a^b g = (h/2)(g a + g b) − (h²/12)(g₁ b − g₁ a)\n                + (1/24)·∫_a^b w″·g₂,      h = b−a\n\nwith NO `g‴` or `g⁗` hypotheses anywhere.  The order-2 rule was originally\nderived from the quartic kernel, which forced four derivatives; but the kernel\nidentity\n\n    w″ = 12(x−a)² − 12(b−a)(x−a) + 2(b−a)² = −12·(x−a)(b−x) + 2(b−a)²\n\nexpresses `w″` in terms of the ORDER-1 kernel `(x−a)(b−x)`, so\n\n    (1/24)∫ w″ g₂ = −½∫ (x−a)(b−x) g₂ + ((b−a)²/12)·∫ g₂\n                  = −½∫ (x−a)(b−x) g₂ + ((b−a)²/12)(g₁ b − g₁ a)\n\nby the FTC on `g₂`.  Substituting into `integral_eq_trapezoid_sub` the `B₂`\nterms cancel exactly and the order-1 identity is recovered.  So the order-2\nrule is a REWRITING of the order-1 rule, not a deeper theorem — and it inherits\nthe order-1 rule's `C²` hypotheses. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.abs_weight_sq_deriv2_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.abs_weight_sq_deriv2_le","kind":"theorem","line":354,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/CorrectedTrapezoid.lean#L354-L364","doc":"`|w″| ≤ 2(b−a)²` on `[a,b]`.  Writing `u = x−a` and `h = b−a`,\n`w″ = 12u² − 12hu + 2h² = 12(u − h/2)² − h²`, which on `u ∈ [0,h]` ranges over\n`[−h², 2h²]`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.integral_weight_sq_mul_deriv4_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.integral_weight_sq_mul_deriv4_eq","kind":"theorem","line":275,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/CorrectedTrapezoid.lean#L275-L344","doc":"**The order-2 remainder in second-derivative form.**\n\n    ∫_a^b (x−a)²(b−x)²·g⁗ = ∫_a^b (12(x−a)² − 12(b−a)(x−a) + 2(b−a)²)·g″\n\nTwo integrations by parts with no boundary terms, because `w` has double roots at\nboth endpoints — done as one FTC application against `F := w·g‴ − w′·g″`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.integral_weight_sq_deriv2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.integral_weight_sq_deriv2","kind":"theorem","line":248,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/CorrectedTrapezoid.lean#L248-L273","doc":"**The order-2 kernel's derivative has zero mean:** `∫_a^b w″ = 0`.\n\nImmediate from the FTC: its antiderivative `w′ = 4(x−a)³ − 6(b−a)(x−a)² +\n2(b−a)²(x−a)` vanishes at both endpoints. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.abs_corrected_trapezoid_sub_integral_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.abs_corrected_trapezoid_sub_integral_le","kind":"theorem","line":169,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/CorrectedTrapezoid.lean#L169-L212","doc":"**The corrected trapezoid error bound.**  `h⁵·M/720` for `|f⁗| ≤ M`.\n\nTwo orders better per cell than the plain trapezoid rule — `O(h⁴)` rather than\n`O(h²)` once summed over `1/h` cells. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.integral_eq_corrected_trapezoid_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.integral_eq_corrected_trapezoid_sub","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/CorrectedTrapezoid.lean#L80-L167","doc":"**The corrected trapezoid rule with its exact error term.**\n\n    ∫_a^b f = (h/2)(f a + f b) − (h²/12)(f′ b − f′ a)\n                + (1/24)·∫_a^b (x−a)²(b−x)²·f⁗(x) dx,     h = b−a\n\nexact for every `f` with four derivatives on `[[a,b]]` and `f⁗` continuous\nthere.  The `−(h²/12)(f′ b − f′ a)` term is the Euler–Maclaurin `B₂`\ncorrection. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.integral_weight_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.integral_weight_sq","kind":"theorem","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/CorrectedTrapezoid.lean#L58-L78","doc":"`∫_a^b (x−a)²(b−x)² dx = (b−a)⁵/30` — the total mass of the order-2 Peano\nkernel.  This is what turns `sup|f⁗|` into the `1/720`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}